          %h2#Idea Idea
          %p
            Generally, the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/propositions"} propositions
            in a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/formal+logic"} formal logic
            may be thought of as having an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebra"} algebraic
            structure with respect to logical operators like “
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/logical+conjunction"}> and
            ” and “
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/logical+disjunction"}> or
            ”. Under this identification, different systems of logic correspond to different sets of operators and axioms.
          %p
            For the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/intuitionistic+logic"} intuitionistic
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/propositional+calculus"} propositional calculus
            = succeed "\u201D," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/logical+conjunction"} and
            = succeed "\u201D," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/logical+disjunction"} or
            = succeed "\u201D," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/true"} true
            = succeed "\u201D," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/false"} false
            = succeed "\u201D." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/implication"} implies
            %strong Heyting algebra
            captures these operators and their axioms, so that a Heyting algebra is precisely a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model"} model
            of the intuitionistic propositional calculus.
          %p
            A Heyting algebra where
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/excluded+middle"} excluded middle
            holds is a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Boolean+algebra"} Boolean algebra
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classical+logic"} classical
            propositional calculus.
          %p
            To model
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantifiers"} quantifiers
            and variables, i.e. to extend from
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/propositional+calculus"} propositional calculus
            to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/first-order+logic"} first-order
            aka
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/predicate+logic"} predicate logic
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/hyperdoctrine"} hyperdoctrine
            on Heyting algebras, called a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/first-order+hyperdoctrine"} first-order hyperdoctrine
          %h2#definition Definition
          %p
          .num_defn
            %h6 Definition
            %p
              A
              %strong Heyting algebra
              is a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/lattice"} lattice
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi L
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L
              which as a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/poset"} poset
              admits an operation of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/implication"} implication
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ⇒
                    %mo :
                    %msup
                      %mi L
                      %mi op
                    %mo ×
                    %mi L
                    %mo →
                    %mi L
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Rightarrow: L^{op} \times L \to L
              satisfying the condition (really a
              = succeed ")" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/universal+property"} universal property
            #eq:UniversalProperty.maruku-equation
              %span.maruku-eq-number (1)
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mo ∧
                    %mi a
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ≤
                    %mi b
                    %mspace{:width => "2em"}
                    %mtext if and only if
                    %mspace{:width => "2em"}
                    %mi x
                    %mo ≤
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi a
                    %mo ⇒
                    %mi b
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    (x \wedge a) \leq b \qquad \text{if and only if} \qquad x \leq (a \Rightarrow b)
            %p
              In other words,
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi a
                    %mo ⇒
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a \Rightarrow {-}
              must be
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/right+adjoint"} right adjoint
              to
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %mo ∧
                      %mi a
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} {-} \wedge a
            %p
          %p This is equivalent to the following definition.
          %p
          .num_defn
            %h6 Definition
            %p
              A
              %strong Heyting algebra
              is a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bicartesian+closed+category"} bicartesian closed
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/poset"} poset
              = succeed ")" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/thin+category"} thin category
            %ul
              %li
                %p
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/finitely+complete+category"}> finitely complete
                  ,
              %li
                %p
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/finitely+cocomplete+category"}> finitely cocomplete
                  ,
              %li
                %p
                  and
                  = succeed "." do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cartesian+closed+category"} cartesian closed
            %p
              The implication
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi a
                    %mo ⇒
                    %mi b
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a\Rightarrow b
              is the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/exponential+object"} exponential object
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi b
                        %mi a
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} b^a
            %p
          %p
            Often (for instance, when doing forcing categorically) one is interested in
            %em complete
            Heyting algebras, which are Heyting algebras that admit
            %em arbitrary
            meets and joins (that is, not necessarily finite).
          %p
          .num_remark
            %h6 Remark
            %p
              Insofar as all these properties of a poset are described by
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/universal+properties"} universal properties
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/property-like+structure"} property-like structure
              on a poset; a poset can be a Heyting algebra in at most one way.
            %p
          %p
            The definition of Heyting algebra may be recast into purely
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebraic+theory"} equational form
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo ⇒
                  %mi y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ∧
                  %mi x
                  %mo ≤
                  %mi y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (x \Rightarrow y) \wedge x \leq y
            and
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi y
                    %mo ≤
                    %mi x
                    %mo ⇒
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi y
                    %mo ∧
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} y \leq x \Rightarrow (y \wedge x)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi a
                  %mo ≤
                  %mi b
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a \leq b
            iff
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi a
                    %mo =
                    %mi a
                    %mo ∧
                    %mi b
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a = a \wedge b
            %strong
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internalization"} internal
              Heyting algebra
            in any
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cartesian+monoidal+category"} category with products
          %p
          .num_defn
            %h6 Definition
            %p
              A Heyting algebra
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homomorphism"} homomorphism
              is a homomorphism of the underlying
              = succeed "s" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/lattice"} lattice
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo ⇒
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Rightarrow
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/concrete+category"} concrete category
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/HeytAlg"} HeytAlg
          %p
            If one relaxes the requirement that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ≤
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \leq
            be antisymmetric, so that instead of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/poset"} poset
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi L
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L
            is only a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/preorder"} preorder
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Heyting+prealgebra"} Heyting prealgebra
          %h2#properties Properties
          %p
          #prop:distributive.num_prop
            %h6 Proposition
            %p
              Any Heyting algebra is a
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/distributive+lattice"} distributive lattice
          %p
          .proof
            %h6 Proof
            %p
              As discussed at
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/distributive+lattice"} distributive lattice
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi x
                      %mo ∧
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi y
                      %mo ∨
                      %mi z
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ≤
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi x
                      %mo ∧
                      %mi y
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ∨
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi x
                      %mo ∧
                      %mi z
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \wedge (y \vee z) \leq (x \wedge y) \vee (x \wedge z)
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/exercise"} exercise
              applying the universal property
              = succeed "." do
                %a.maruku-eqref{:href => "#eq:UniversalProperty"} (1)
            %p
              More abstractly: the distributivity law
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                    %mo ∧
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi y
                    %mo ∨
                    %mi z
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mo ∧
                    %mi y
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ∨
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mo ∧
                    %mi z
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \wedge (y \vee z) = (x \wedge y) \vee (x \wedge z)
              says precisely that
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                    %mo ∧
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \wedge {-}
              preserves binary joins, which it does because it has a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/right+adjoint"} right adjoint
              (namely
              = succeed ")" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi x
                      %mo ⇒
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \Rightarrow {-}
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/left+adjoints+preserve+colimits"} preserves all colimits
          %p
            The relation of
            %strong modus ponens
            is immediate from
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.maruku-eqref{:href => "#eq:UniversalProperty"} (1)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi a
                  %mo ,
                  %mi b
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a, b
            in a Heyting algebra,
          #eq:ModusPonens.maruku-equation
            %span.maruku-eq-number (2)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi a
                  %mo ∧
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi a
                  %mo ⇒
                  %mi b
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ≤
                  %mi b
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  a \wedge (a \Rightarrow b) \leq b
          %p We list some further basic facts which are frequently useful, including in the proofs below:
          %p
          #BasicProperties.num_prop
            %h6 Proposition
            %p
              The following hold for any
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi a
                    %mo ,
                    %mi b
                    %mo ,
                    %mi c
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a, b, c
              in any Heyting algebra:
            %ol
              %li
                %p
                  Composition law:
                  = succeed "." do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi a
                          %mo ⇒
                          %mi b
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mo ∧
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi b
                          %mo ⇒
                          %mi c
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mo ≤
                          %mi a
                          %mo ⇒
                          %mi c
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (a \Rightarrow b) \wedge (b \Rightarrow c) \leq a \Rightarrow c
              %li
                %p
                  Currying:
                  = succeed "." do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi a
                          %mo ∧
                          %mi b
                          %mo ⇒
                          %mi c
                          %mo =
                          %mi a
                          %mo ⇒
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi b
                          %mo ⇒
                          %mi c
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                          a \wedge b \Rightarrow c =
                          a \Rightarrow (b \Rightarrow c)
              %li
                %p
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi a
                        %mo ⇒
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a \Rightarrow {-}
                  is monotone:
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi a
                        %mo ⇒
                        %mi b
                        %mo ≤
                        %mi a
                        %mo ⇒
                        %mi c
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a \Rightarrow b \leq a \Rightarrow c
                  if
                  = succeed "." do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi b
                          %mo ≤
                          %mi c
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} b \leq c
              %li
                %p
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi a
                        %mo ⇒
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a \Rightarrow {-}
                  is increasing:
                  = succeed "." do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi b
                          %mo ≤
                          %mi a
                          %mo ⇒
                          %mi b
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} b \leq a \Rightarrow b
              %li
                %p
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi a
                        %mo ⇒
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a \Rightarrow {-}
                  is idempotent:
                  = succeed "." do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi a
                          %mo ⇒
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi a
                          %mo ⇒
                          %mi b
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mo =
                          %mi a
                          %mo ⇒
                          %mi b
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a \Rightarrow (a \Rightarrow b) = a \Rightarrow b
              %li
                %p
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                        %mo ⇒
                        %mi a
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} {-} \Rightarrow a
                  is antimonotone:
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi c
                        %mo ⇒
                        %mi a
                        %mo ≤
                        %mi b
                        %mo ⇒
                        %mi a
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} c \Rightarrow a \leq b \Rightarrow a
                  if
                  = succeed "." do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi b
                          %mo ≤
                          %mi c
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} b \leq c
              %li
                %p
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                        %mo ⇒
                        %mi a
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} {-} \Rightarrow a
                  is self-adjoint:
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                        %mo ⇒
                        %mi a
                        %mo :
                        %mi L
                        %mo →
                        %msup
                          %mi L
                          %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} op
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} {-} \Rightarrow a: L \rightarrow L^{\mathrm{op}}
                  is left adjoint to
                  = succeed "." do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                          %mo ⇒
                          %mi a
                          %mo :
                          %msup
                            %mi L
                            %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} op
                          %mo →
                          %mi L
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} {-} \Rightarrow a: L^{\mathrm{op}} \rightarrow L
                %p
                  Explicitly,
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi b
                        %mo ≤
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi c
                        %mo ⇒
                        %mi a
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} b \leq (c \Rightarrow a)
                  just if
                  = succeed "," do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi b
                          %mo ⇒
                          %mi a
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %msup
                            %mo ≤
                            %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} op
                          %mi c
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (b \Rightarrow a) \leq^{\mathrm{op}} c
                  = succeed "." do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi c
                          %mo ≤
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi b
                          %mo ⇒
                          %mi a
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} c \leq (b \Rightarrow a)
              %li
                %p
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                        %mo ⇒
                        %mi a
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo ⇒
                        %mi a
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} ({-} \Rightarrow a) \Rightarrow a
                  is increasing:
                  = succeed "." do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi b
                          %mo ≤
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi b
                          %mo ⇒
                          %mi a
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mo ⇒
                          %mi a
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                          b \leq
                          (b \Rightarrow a) \Rightarrow a
              %li
                %p
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                        %mo ⇒
                        %mi a
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo ⇒
                        %mi a
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} ({-} \Rightarrow a) \Rightarrow a
                  is idempotent:
                  = succeed "." do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi b
                          %mo ⇒
                          %mi a
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mo ⇒
                          %mi a
                          %mo =
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi b
                          %mo ⇒
                          %mi a
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mo ⇒
                          %mi a
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mo ⇒
                          %mi a
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mo ⇒
                          %mi a
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                          (b \Rightarrow a) \Rightarrow a =
                          (((b \Rightarrow a) \Rightarrow a) \Rightarrow a) \Rightarrow a
            %p
          .proof
            %h6 Proof
            %p
              Each of these is a short exercise using the universal property
              %a.maruku-eqref{:href => "#eq:UniversalProperty"} (1)
              and modus ponens
              = succeed "." do
                %a.maruku-eqref{:href => "#eq:ModusPonens"} (2)
          %p Most of these facts can be packaged up more abstractly like so:
          %p
          #Monads.num_prop
            %h6 Proposition
            %p
              In any Heyting algebra
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi H
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H
              = succeed ":" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi a
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi H
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a \in H
            %ul
              %li
                %p
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi a
                        %mo ⇒
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a \Rightarrow {-}
                  is a
                  = succeed "." do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monad"} monad
              %li
                %p
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                        %mo ⇒
                        %mi a
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo ⇒
                        %mi a
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} ({-} \Rightarrow a) \Rightarrow a
                  is a monad.
            %p
          .proof
            %h6 Proof
            %p
              A monad in a poset (or a
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/preorder"} preorder
              = succeed ")" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%280%2C1%29-category"} (0,1)-category
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi H
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H
              is just a monotone, increasing, idempotent function.
            %p
              From Proposition
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#BasicProperties"}
              it is immediate that this describes both
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi a
                    %mo ⇒
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a \Rightarrow {-}
              and
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %mo ⇒
                      %mi a
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ⇒
                      %mi a
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} ({-} \Rightarrow a) \Rightarrow a
          %h3#negation Negation
          %p
            In any Heyting algebra
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi H
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/negation"} negation
            operator:
          %p
          #Negation.num_defn
            %h6 Definition
            %p
              The
              %strong negation
              operator
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ¬
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %msup
                      %mi H
                      %mi op
                    %mo →
                    %mi H
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \neg\colon H^{op} \to H
              is
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo ¬
                      %mi x
                      %mo =
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi x
                      %mo ⇒
                      %mn 0
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \neg x = (x \Rightarrow 0)
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 0
              is the bottom element of the lattice.
          %p
          #DoubleNegationMonad.num_cor
            %h6 Corollary
            %p
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ¬
                    %mo ¬
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mi H
                    %mo →
                    %mi H
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \neg\neg\colon H \to H
              is a
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monad"} monad
          .proof
            %h6 Proof
            %p
              Immediate from
              = succeed "." do
                %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#Monads"}
          %p We collect some further frequently-useful facts:
          %p
          #NegationFacts.num_prop
            %h6 Proposition
            %p
              In any Heyting algebra
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi H
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H
              = succeed ":" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi x
                      %mo ,
                      %mi y
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi H
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x, y \in H
            %ol
              %li
                %p
                  Negation is antimonotone:
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo ¬
                        %mi y
                        %mo ≤
                        %mo ¬
                        %mi x
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \neg y \leq \neg x
                  if
                  = succeed "." do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi x
                          %mo ≤
                          %mi y
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \leq y
              %li
                %p
                  Currying:
                  = succeed "." do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mo ¬
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi x
                          %mo ∧
                          %mi y
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mo =
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi x
                          %mo ⇒
                          %mo ¬
                          %mi y
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \neg (x \wedge y) = (x \Rightarrow \neg y)
              %li
                %p
                  Triple negation is just negation:
                  = succeed "." do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mo ¬
                          %mo ¬
                          %mo ¬
                          %mi x
                          %mo =
                          %mo ¬
                          %mi x
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \neg \neg \neg x = \neg x
              %li
                %p
                  Proof by contradiction:
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi x
                        %mo ≤
                        %mo ¬
                        %mi y
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \leq \neg y
                  just if
                  = succeed "." do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi x
                          %mo ∧
                          %mi y
                          %mo ≤
                          %mn 0
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \wedge y \leq 0
              %li
                %p
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/De+Morgan%27s+law"} De Morgan's law
                  for negation over disjunction:
                %p
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo ¬
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi x
                        %mo ∨
                        %mi y
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo =
                        %mo ¬
                        %mi x
                        %mo ∧
                        %mo ¬
                        %mi y
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \neg (x \vee y) = \neg x \wedge \neg y
                  \.
              %li
                %p
                  One-way De Morgan’s law for negation over conjunction:
                  = succeed "." do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mo ¬
                          %mi x
                          %mo ∨
                          %mo ¬
                          %mi y
                          %mo ≤
                          %mo ¬
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi x
                          %mo ∧
                          %mi y
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \neg x \vee \neg y \leq \neg (x \wedge y)
              %li
                %p
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi x
                        %mo ∧
                        %mo ¬
                        %mi x
                        %mo =
                        %mn 0
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \wedge \neg x = 0
              %li
                %p
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo ¬
                        %mo ¬
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi x
                        %mo ∨
                        %mo ¬
                        %mi x
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo =
                        %mn 1
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \neg \neg (x \vee \neg x) = 1
                  (equivalently,
                  = succeed ")" do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mo ¬
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi x
                          %mo ∨
                          %mo ¬
                          %mi x
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mo =
                          %mn 0
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \neg (x \vee \neg x) = 0
              %li
                %p
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo ¬
                        %mi x
                        %mo ∨
                        %mi y
                        %mo ≤
                        %mi x
                        %mo ⇒
                        %mi y
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \neg x \vee y \leq x \Rightarrow y
              %li
                #eq:NegIfEq.maruku-equation
                  %span.maruku-eq-number (3)
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo ¬
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi x
                        %mo ⇒
                        %mi y
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo =
                        %mo ¬
                        %mo ¬
                        %mi x
                        %mo ∧
                        %mo ¬
                        %mi y
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                        \\neg (x \Rightarrow y) = \neg\neg x \wedge \neg y
            %p
          .proof
            %h6 Proof
            %p
              Each of these is a short
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/exercise"} exercise
              using the universal property
              = succeed "," do
                %a.maruku-eqref{:href => "#eq:UniversalProperty"} (1)
              = succeed "," do
                %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#BasicProperties"}
          %p
            Several of the facts in
            %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#NegationFacts"}
            are weakenings of familiar propositional identities from
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classical+logic"} classical logic
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                A Heyting algebra where the remaining
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/De+Morgan+law"} De Morgan law
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo ¬
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi x
                      %mo ∧
                      %mi y
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo =
                      %mo ¬
                      %mi x
                      %mo ∨
                      %mo ¬
                      %mi y
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \neg (x \wedge y) = \neg x \vee \neg y
                holds is precisely a
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/De+Morgan+Heyting+algebra"} De Morgan Heyting algebra
            %li
              %p
                A Heyting algebra satisfying any of the following (equivalent) conditions is precisely a
                = succeed ":" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Boolean+algebra"} Boolean algebra
              %ul
                %li
                  %p
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mo ∀
                          %mi x
                          %mo .
                          %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                          %mo ¬
                          %mo ¬
                          %mi x
                          %mo =
                          %mi x
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \forall x.\; \neg\neg x = x
                %li
                  %p
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mo ∀
                          %mi x
                          %mo .
                          %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                          %mi x
                          %mo ∨
                          %mo ¬
                          %mi x
                          %mo =
                          %mn 1
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \forall x.\; x \vee \neg x = 1
                %li
                  %p
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mo ∀
                          %mi x
                          %mo ,
                          %mi y
                          %mo .
                          %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                          %mi x
                          %mo ⇒
                          %mi y
                          %mo =
                          %mo ¬
                          %mi x
                          %mo ∨
                          %mi y
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \forall x, y.\; x \Rightarrow y = \neg x \vee y
              %p These also imply the De Morgan law.
          %h3#double_negation Double negation
          %p
            The double-negation map
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ¬
                  %mo ¬
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mi H
                  %mo →
                  %mi H
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \neg\neg \colon H \to H
            on a Heyting algebra
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi H
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H
            is often relevant, particularly in the relationship
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "#ToBooleanAlgebras"} with Boolean algebras
            %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#DoubleNegationMonad"}
            that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ¬
                  %mo ¬
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \neg\neg
            is a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monad"} monad
          %p
          #lem:NegnegMeets.num_lemma
            %h6 Lemma
            %p
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/double+negation"} Double negation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ¬
                    %mo ¬
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mi L
                    %mo →
                    %mi L
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \neg \neg\colon L \to L
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/preserved+limit"} preserves
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/finite+limit"} finite
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/meets"} meets
          %p
          .proof
            %h6 Proof
            %p
              Nullary meets are trivial:
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo ¬
                      %mo ¬
                      %mn 1
                      %mo =
                      %mo ¬
                      %mn 0
                      %mo =
                      %mn 1
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \neg \neg 1 = \neg 0 = 1
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ¬
                    %mo ¬
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mo ∧
                    %mi y
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ≤
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mo ¬
                    %mo ¬
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ∧
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mo ¬
                    %mo ¬
                    %mi y
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \neg \neg (x \wedge y) \leq (\neg \neg x) \wedge (\neg \neg y)
              holds simply because
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ¬
                    %mo ¬
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \neg \neg
              is monotone.
            %p
              In the other direction, we show
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mo ¬
                    %mo ¬
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ∧
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mo ¬
                    %mo ¬
                    %mi y
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ≤
                    %mo ¬
                    %mo ¬
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mo ∧
                    %mi y
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    (\neg \neg x) \wedge (\neg \neg y) \leq \neg \neg (x \wedge y)
              by currying (per
              = succeed ")" do
                %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#NegationFacts"}
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ¬
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mo ∧
                    %mi y
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \neg (x \wedge y)
              to calculate:
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mrow
                      %mtable{:columnalign => "right left right left right left right left right left", :columnspacing => "0em", :displaystyle => "true"}
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mo ¬
                            %mo ¬
                            %mi x
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                            %mo ∧
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mo ¬
                            %mo ¬
                            %mi y
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                            %mo ∧
                            %mo ¬
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mi x
                            %mo ∧
                            %mi y
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mtd
                            %mo =
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mo ¬
                            %mo ¬
                            %mi x
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                            %mo ∧
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mo ¬
                            %mi y
                            %mo ⇒
                            %mn 0
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                            %mo ∧
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mi x
                            %mo ⇒
                            %mo ¬
                            %mi y
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mo ≤
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mo ¬
                            %mo ¬
                            %mi x
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                            %mo ∧
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mi x
                            %mo ⇒
                            %mn 0
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mo =
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mo ¬
                            %mi x
                            %mo ⇒
                            %mn 0
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                            %mo ∧
                            %mo ¬
                            %mi x
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mo ≤
                            %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\begin{aligned}
                    (\neg \neg x) \wedge (\neg \neg y) \wedge \neg (x \wedge y)
                    \&amp; =
                    (\neg \neg x) \wedge (\neg y \Rightarrow 0) \wedge (x \Rightarrow \neg y)
                    \\\
                    \&amp; \leq
                    (\neg \neg x) \wedge (x \Rightarrow 0)
                    \\\
                    \&amp; =
                    (\neg x \Rightarrow 0) \wedge \neg x
                    \\\
                    \&amp; \leq
                    0
                    \\end{aligned}
            %p
              where the two inequalities follow from composition (Proposition
              = succeed ")" do
                %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#BasicProperties"}
              = succeed "." do
                %a.maruku-eqref{:href => "#eq:ModusPonens"} (2)
          %p
            The following Lemma
            %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#lem:NegnegImplication"}
            is important for the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/double+negation+translation"} double negation translation
          %p
          #lem:NegnegImplication.num_lemma
            %h6 Lemma
            %p
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/double+negation"} Double negation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ¬
                    %mo ¬
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mi L
                    %mo →
                    %mi L
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \neg \neg\colon L \to L
              preserves
              = succeed ":" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/implication"} implication
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo ¬
                      %mo ¬
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi a
                      %mo ⇒
                      %mi b
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo =
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mo ¬
                      %mo ¬
                      %mi a
                      %mo ⇒
                      %mo ¬
                      %mo ¬
                      %mi b
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                      \\neg\neg( a \Rightarrow b ) =
                      (\neg\neg a \Rightarrow \neg\neg b)
          .proof
            %h6 Proof
            %p
              Applying
              %a.maruku-eqref{:href => "#eq:NegIfEq"} (3)
              and currying (by
              = succeed ")," do
                %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#NegationFacts"}
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ¬
                    %mo ¬
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi a
                    %mo ⇒
                    %mi b
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %mo ¬
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mo ¬
                    %mo ¬
                    %mi a
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ∧
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mo ¬
                    %mi b
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mo ¬
                    %mo ¬
                    %mi a
                    %mo ⇒
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mo ¬
                    %mi b
                    %mo ⇒
                    %mn 0
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mo ¬
                    %mo ¬
                    %mi a
                    %mo ⇒
                    %mo ¬
                    %mo ¬
                    %mi b
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\neg\neg (a \Rightarrow b) =
                    \\neg ((\neg\neg a) \wedge (\neg b)) =
                    (\neg\neg a \Rightarrow (\neg b \Rightarrow 0)) =
                    (\neg \neg a \Rightarrow \neg \neg b)
                    \\; .
            %p
          %h2#relation_to_other_concepts Relation to other concepts
          %h3#to_toposes To toposes
          %p
            An
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/elementary+topos"} elementary topos
            is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vertical+categorification"} vertical categorification
            of a Heyting algebra: the notion of Heyting algebra is essentially equivalent to that of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%280%2C1%29-topos"} (0,1)-topos
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Grothendieck+topos"} Grothendieck
            = succeed "-topos" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 0
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (0,1)
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/frame"} frame
            or
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/locale"} locale
          %p
            In a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Heyting+category"} Heyting category
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/subobject+poset"} subobject poset
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Sub
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi A
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Sub(A)
            is a Heyting algebra. In particular, this holds for every
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topos"} topos
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/power+object"} power object
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝒫
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi A
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{P}(A)
            is an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internalisation"} internal
            Heyting algebra that corresponds to the external Heyting algebra
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Sub
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi A
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Sub(A)
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/boolean+topos"} boolean topos
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/boolean+algebras"} boolean algebras
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internal+logic"} internal logic
            of a topos (or other Heyting category) is intuitionistic.
          %p
            The proof of Lemma
            %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#lem:NegnegMeets"}
            can be made purely equational, and is therefore internally valid in any category with products. Applied to the internal Heyting algebra
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi L
                  %mo =
                  %mi Ω
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L = \Omega
            of a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topos"} topos
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/subobject+classifier"} subobject classifier
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ¬
                  %mo ¬
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mi Ω
                  %mo →
                  %mi Ω
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \neg \neg\colon \Omega \to \Omega
            defines a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lawvere%E2%80%93Tierney+topology"} Lawvere–Tierney topology
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/double+negation+sublocale"} double negation sublocale
            of any
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/locale"} locale
          %h3#to_topology To topology
          %p
            One of the chief sources of Heyting algebras is given by
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topology"} topologies
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/complete+lattice"} complete lattice
            (it has arbitrary
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/joins"} joins
            and
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/meets"} meets
            = succeed ")," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/intersection"} intersection
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi U
                  %mo ⇒
                  %mi V
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mi int
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msup
                    %mi U
                    %mi c
                  %mo ∨
                  %mi V
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (U \Rightarrow V) = int(U^c \vee V)
          %p
            where
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi U
                  %mo ,
                  %mi V
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U, V
            are open sets,
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi U
                    %mi c
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U^c
            is the set-theoretic complement of
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi U
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi int
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi S
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} int(S)
            denotes the interior of a subset
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi S
                    %mo ⊆
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S \subseteq X
          %p
            Somewhat more generally, a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/frame"} frame
            (a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sup-lattice"} sup-lattice
            in which finite meets distribute over arbitrary sups) also carries a Heyting algebra structure. In a frame, we may define
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi u
                  %mo ⇒
                  %mi v
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %munder
                    %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ⋁
                    %mrow
                      %mi x
                      %mo ∧
                      %mi u
                      %mo ≤
                      %mi v
                  %mi x
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (u \Rightarrow v) = \bigvee_{x \wedge u \leq v} x
          %p
            and the distributivity property guarantees that the universal property
            %a.maruku-eqref{:href => "#eq:UniversalProperty"} (1)
            holds. (The detailed proof is a “baby” application of an
            = succeed ".)" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/adjoint+functor+theorem"} adjoint functor theorem
          %p
            Thus frames are extensionally the same thing as
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/complete+lattice"} complete
                Heyting algebras
            %em intensionally
            they are quite different; that is, a morphism of frames is not usually a morphism of complete Heyting algebras: they do not preserve the implication operator.
          %p
            A
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/locale"} locale
            is the same thing as a frame, but again the morphisms are different; they are reversed.
          %p
            Topologies that are
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Boolean+algebras"} Boolean algebras
            are the exception rather than the rule; basic examples include topologies of
            = succeed ";" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Stone+spaces"} Stone spaces
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Stone+duality"} Stone duality
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/discrete+space"} discrete space
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
          %h2#ToBooleanAlgebras Adjunctions with Boolean algebras
          %p
            There are several ways of passing back and forth between Boolean algebras and Heyting algebras, having to do with the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/double+negation"} double negation
            operator. By
            = succeed "," do
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#DoubleNegationMonad"}
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ¬
                  %mo ¬
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mi L
                  %mo →
                  %mi L
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \neg \neg\colon L \to L
            is a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monad"} monad
            = succeed "," do
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#lem:NegnegMeets"}
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/preserved+limit"} preserves
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/finite+limit"} finite
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/meets"} meets
          %p
            Now let
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi L
                    %mrow
                      %mo ¬
                      %mo ¬
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L_{\neg\neg}
            denote the poset of
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/regular+element"}> regular element
              s
            of
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi L
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
            such that
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ¬
                    %mo ¬
                    %mi x
                    %mo =
                    %mi x
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \neg\neg x = x
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi L
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L
            is the topology of a space, an open set
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi U
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U
            is
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/regular+open+subspace"} regular
            if and only if it is the interior of its closure, that is if it is a regular element of the Heyting algebra of open sets described above.) With the help of Lemma
            = succeed "," do
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#lem:NegnegMeets"}
          %p
          #thm:NegnegAdjoint.num_theorem
            %h6 Theorem
            %p
              The poset
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi L
                      %mrow
                        %mo ¬
                        %mo ¬
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L_{\neg\neg}
              is a Boolean algebra. Moreover, the assignment
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi L
                    %mo ↦
                    %msub
                      %mi L
                      %mrow
                        %mo ¬
                        %mo ¬
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L \mapsto L_{\neg\neg}
              is the object part of a functor
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi F
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mi Heyt
                    %mo →
                    %mi Bool
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F\colon Heyt \to Bool
            %p
              called
              = succeed "," do
                %em Booleanization
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi i
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mi Bool
                    %mo ↪
                    %mi Heyt
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i\colon Bool \hookrightarrow Heyt.
            %p
              The unit of the
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/adjoint+functor"} adjunction
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi L
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi L
                    %mo →
                    %msub
                      %mi L
                      %mrow
                        %mo ¬
                        %mo ¬
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L \to L_{\neg\neg}
              which maps each element
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
              to its
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/regular+element"} regularization
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo ¬
                      %mo ¬
                      %mi x
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \neg\neg x
          %p
          .proof
            %h6 Proof
            %p
              To avoid confusion from two different maps called
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ¬
                    %mo ¬
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \neg\neg
              that differ only in their codomain, write
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi M
                    %mo :
                    %mi L
                    %mo →
                    %mi L
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M: L \rightarrow L
              for the monad and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi U
                    %mo :
                    %mi L
                    %mo →
                    %msub
                      %mi L
                      %mrow
                        %mo ¬
                        %mo ¬
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U: L \rightarrow L_{\neg\neg}
              for the left adjoint. Write
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ι
                    %mo :
                    %msub
                      %mi L
                      %mrow
                        %mo ¬
                        %mo ¬
                    %mo →
                    %mi L
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \iota: L_{\neg\neg} \rightarrow L
              for the right adjoint, so that
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi M
                      %mo =
                      %mi ι
                      %mo ∘
                      %mi U
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M = \iota \circ U
            %p
              We first show that
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi L
                      %mrow
                        %mo ¬
                        %mo ¬
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L_{\neg\neg}
              is a Heyting algebra and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi U
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U
              is a Heyting-algebra map. Because
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi U
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U
              is surjective (and monotone), it suffices to show that it preserves the Heyting-algebra operators: finite meets, finite joins, and implication.
            %p
              Because
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ι
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \iota
              is full, it reflects meets. Therefore because by Lemma
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#lem:NegnegMeets"}
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi M
                    %mo =
                    %mi ι
                    %mo ∘
                    %mi U
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M = \iota \circ U
              preserves finite meets, so too does
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi U
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U
            %p
              Finally, for
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi a
                      %mo ,
                      %mi b
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi L
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a, b \in L
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo ¬
                      %mo ¬
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi a
                      %mo ⇒
                      %mi b
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \neg\neg (a \Rightarrow b)
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#lem:NegnegImplication"}
              is the
              = succeed "-implication" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi L
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo ¬
                      %mo ¬
                      %mi a
                      %mo ⇒
                      %mo ¬
                      %mo ¬
                      %mi b
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \neg\neg a \Rightarrow \neg\neg b
              %a.maruku-eqref{:href => "#eq:UniversalProperty"} (1)
              also in
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi L
                        %mrow
                          %mo ¬
                          %mo ¬
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L_{\neg\neg}
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi x
                      %mo ∈
                      %msub
                        %mi L
                        %mrow
                          %mo ¬
                          %mo ¬
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \in L_{\neg\neg}
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ι
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ≤
                    %mo ¬
                    %mo ¬
                    %mi a
                    %msub
                      %mo ⇒
                      %mi L
                    %mo ¬
                    %mo ¬
                    %mi b
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \iota(x) \leq \neg\neg a \Rightarrow_L \neg\neg b
              just if
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ι
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %msub
                      %mo ∧
                      %mi L
                    %mo ¬
                    %mo ¬
                    %mi a
                    %mo ≤
                    %mo ¬
                    %mo ¬
                    %mi b
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \iota(x) \wedge_L \neg\neg a \leq \neg\neg b
              by the universal property in
              = succeed ";" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi L
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ι
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \iota
              reflects meets this is equivalent to
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi x
                      %msub
                        %mo ∧
                        %mrow
                          %msub
                            %mi L
                            %mrow
                              %mo ¬
                              %mo ¬
                      %mo ¬
                      %mo ¬
                      %mi a
                      %mo ≤
                      %mo ¬
                      %mo ¬
                      %mi b
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \wedge_{L_{\neg\neg}} \neg\neg a \leq \neg\neg b
            %p
              Now because
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi U
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U
              preserves implication and (the empty join)
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mn 0
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 0
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ¬
                    %mo ¬
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \neg\neg
              in
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi L
                      %mrow
                        %mo ¬
                        %mo ¬
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L_{\neg\neg}
              is the identity, so the latter is a Boolean algebra.
            %p
              Therefore
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi U
                    %mo =
                    %mo ¬
                    %mo ¬
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mi L
                    %mo →
                    %msub
                      %mi L
                      %mrow
                        %mo ¬
                        %mo ¬
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U = \neg\neg \colon L \to L_{\neg\neg}
              is a Heyting algebra quotient which is the coequalizer of
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mn 1
                      %mo ,
                      %mo ¬
                      %mo ¬
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                      %mi L
                      %mover
                        %mo →
                        %mo →
                      %mi L
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1, \neg\neg \colon L \stackrel{\to}{\to} L
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi L
                    %mo →
                    %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L \to B
              to any Boolean algebra
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi B
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ¬
                    %mo ¬
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \neg\neg
              coincide, factors uniquely through this coequalizer, and the induced map
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi L
                      %mrow
                        %mo ¬
                        %mo ¬
                    %mo →
                    %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L_{\neg \neg} \to B
              is a Boolean algebra map. In other words,
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ¬
                    %mo ¬
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mi L
                    %mo →
                    %msub
                      %mi L
                      %mrow
                        %mo ¬
                        %mo ¬
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \neg\neg \colon L \to L_{\neg\neg}
              is the universal Heyting algebra map to a Boolean algebra, which establishes the adjunction.
          %p
            Thus
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ¬
                  %mo ¬
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mi L
                  %mo →
                  %msub
                    %mi L
                    %mrow
                      %mo ¬
                      %mo ¬
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \neg\neg\colon L \to L_{\neg\neg}
            preserves finite joins and finite meets and implication. In the other direction, we have an inclusion
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi i
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %msub
                      %mi L
                      %mrow
                        %mo ¬
                        %mo ¬
                    %mo →
                    %mi L
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i\colon L_{\neg\neg} \to L
          %p
            Regular elements are not to be confused with
            = succeed "," do
              %em
                %span.newWikiWord>
                  complemented element
                  %a{:href => "/nlab/new/complemented+element"} ?
                s
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
            in a Heyting algebra such that
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                    %mo ∨
                    %mo ¬
                    %mi x
                    %mo =
                    %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \vee \neg x = 1
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 0
                  %mo ,
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (0, 1)
            as an element of the topology of
            = succeed ";" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℝ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/clopen+subset"} clopen subset
          %p
            Complemented elements furnish another universal relation between Boolean algebras and Heyting algebras: the set of complemented elements in a Heyting algebra
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi H
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H
            is a Boolean algebra
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Comp
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi H
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Comp(H)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Comp
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi H
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo →
                  %mi H
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Comp(H) \to H
            is a Heyting algebra map which is universal among Heyting algebra maps
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi B
                  %mo →
                  %mi H
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B \to H
            out of Boolean algebras
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B
          %p
          #thm:CompAdjoint.num_theorem
            %h6 Theorem
            %p
              The assignment
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi H
                    %mo ↦
                    %mi Comp
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi H
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H \mapsto Comp(H)
              is the object part of a right adjoint to the forgetful functor
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Bool
                      %mo →
                      %mi Heyt
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Bool \to Heyt
          %p
          .proof
            %h6 Proof
            %p
              In a Heyting algebra
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi H
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 0
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1
              are clearly complemented. If
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} y
              are complemented, then so are
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi x
                      %mo ∧
                      %mi y
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \wedge y
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi x
                      %mo ∨
                      %mi y
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \vee y
              = succeed ";" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi x
                      %mo ⇒
                      %mi y
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \Rightarrow y
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/exercise"} exercise
              applying
              = succeed "," do
                %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#NegationFacts"}
              = succeed "):" do
                %a.maruku-eqref{:href => "#eq:NegIfEq"} (3)
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mrow
                      %mtable{:columnalign => "right left right left right left right left right left", :columnspacing => "0em", :displaystyle => "true"}
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mi x
                            %mo ⇒
                            %mi y
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                            %mo ∨
                            %mo ¬
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mi x
                            %mo ⇒
                            %mi y
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mtd
                            %mo =
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mi x
                            %mo ⇒
                            %mi y
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                            %mo ∨
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mo ¬
                            %mo ¬
                            %mi x
                            %mo ∧
                            %mo ¬
                            %mi y
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mo =
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mi x
                            %mo ⇒
                            %mi y
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                            %mo ∨
                            %mo ¬
                            %mo ¬
                            %mi x
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                            %mo ∧
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mi x
                            %mo ⇒
                            %mi y
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                            %mo ∨
                            %mo ¬
                            %mi y
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mo ≥
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mo ¬
                            %mi x
                            %mo ∨
                            %mi x
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                            %mo ∧
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mi y
                            %mo ∨
                            %mo ¬
                            %mi y
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mo =
                            %mn 1
                            %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                            %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\begin{aligned}
                    (x \Rightarrow y) \vee \neg(x \Rightarrow y)
                    \&amp;= (x \Rightarrow y) \vee (\neg\neg x \wedge \neg y)
                    \\\
                    \&amp;= ((x \Rightarrow y) \vee \neg\neg x)
                    \\wedge ((x \Rightarrow y) \vee \neg y)
                    \\\
                    \&amp;\geq (\neg x \vee x)
                    \\wedge (y \vee \neg y)
                    \\\
                    \&amp;= 1
                    \\; .
                    \\end{aligned}
            %p
              Thus the complemented elements form a Heyting subalgebra
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Comp
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi H
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ↪
                      %mi H
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Comp(H) \hookrightarrow H
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Comp
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi H
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Comp(H)
              is a Boolean algebra, and clearly if
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B
              is Boolean, then any Heyting algebra map
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi B
                    %mo →
                    %mi H
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B \to H
              factors uniquely through
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Comp
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi H
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ↪
                      %mi H
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Comp(H) \hookrightarrow H
          %h2#examples Examples
          .num_prop
            %h6#proposition Proposition
            %p
              For
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒯
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{T}
              a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topos"} topos
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi 𝒯
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X \in \mathcal{T}
              any
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/object"} object
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Sub
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Sub(X)
              of
              = succeed "s" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/subobject"} subobject
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              is a Heyting algebra.
            %p
              In other words, every topos is a
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Heyting+category"} Heyting category
          %p
            In particular for
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                  %mo =
                  %mi Ω
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X = \Omega
            the
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/subobject+classifier"} subobject classifier
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Sub
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi Ω
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Sub(\Omega)
            is a Heyting algebra.
          %p
            In
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝒯
                  %mo =
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{T} =
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Set"} Set
            for every set
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi S
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
            we have that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Sub
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi S
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Sub(S)
            is the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Boolean+algebra"} Boolean algebra
            of subset of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi S
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
          %p
            More details and examples are spelled out at
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internal+logic#examples"} internal logic#examples
          .num_prop
            %h6#proposition_2 Proposition
            %p
              A
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/frame"} frame
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi L
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L
              is a Heyting algebra.
          .proof
            %h6#proof Proof
            %p
              By the
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/adjoint+functor+theorem"} adjoint functor theorem
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                    %mo ⇒
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \Rightarrow -
              to the map
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                    %mo ∧
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mo :
                    %mi L
                    %mo →
                    %mi L
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \wedge -: L \to L
              exists since this map preserves arbitrary joins.
          %h2#see_also See also
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Heyting+algebra+object"} Heyting algebra object
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/locally+Heyting-algebraic+2-poset"} locally Heyting-algebraic 2-poset
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/implicative+structure"} implicative structure
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sigma-complete+Heyting+algebra"} sigma-complete Heyting algebra
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/complete+Heyting+algebra"} complete Heyting algebra
          %h2#references References
          %p The original reference:
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Arend+Heyting"}> Arend Heyting
                ,
                %em Die formalen Regeln der intuitionistischen Logik. I, II, III.
                Sitzungsberichte der Preußischen Akademie der Wissenschaften, Physikalisch-Mathematische Klasse (1930) 42-56, 57-71, 158-169
              %p abridged reprint in:
              %p
                Karel Berka, Lothar Kreiser (eds.),
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Logik-Texte
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1515/9783112645826"} doi:10.1515/9783112645826
          %p A quick introduction can be found in §1.2 of
          %ul
            %li
